\alttext(Actual interval zero to nine, numerator twelve, denominator eighteen, offset six and observed value thirteen.)

> Derive the estimate and the search invariant

For a guarded nonflat interval [lo,hi], solve the line through its endpoint
values for the key level, then floor its index displacement:

$$
pos=lo+\left\lfloor\frac{(key-a_{lo})(hi-lo)}{a_{hi}-a_{lo}}\right\rfloor.
$$
\alttext(The next candidate is the lower endpoint plus the exact floored fractional index displacement.)

The guards assert a[lo]≤key≤a[hi]. Thus numerator and denominator are
nonnegative, the denominator is positive in the nonflat case, and the offset
lies in [0,hi−lo]. The candidate is safe without a floating clamp. If its value
is below the key, sortedness excludes every index through pos; otherwise, after
an unsuccessful equality test, it excludes every index from pos onward. Updating
to pos+1 or pos−1 strictly shrinks the interval, proving termination in at most
n logical candidate probes. A successful duplicate is the first encountered
candidate, not a promised first or last occurrence.

Equal endpoints require no division: under the guards every value in the sorted
interval equals the key, and lo is returned as one logical probe. An
out-of-range key can be rejected with zero candidate probes even though
endpoints were read. This probe metric is not a physical memory-access or
cache-event count.

The old implementation subtracted int values before widening, causing undefined
signed overflow on valid extreme inputs. It also used a floating fraction whose
rounding could cross a floor boundary. The shared kernel now widens differences
before subtraction and computes the product/division exactly. Corrected probe
sequences and duplicate choices may differ at those former rounded boundaries.
The public function signature and SearchResult field layout are preserved.

Sortedness remains an unchecked precondition. Searching already sorted data uses
constant ordinary workspace and no allocation; tracing adds one owned scalar
record per actual probe. No O(n) validation prescan is hidden in the search
cost. The plotting helper separately validates the complete displayed array.

> Preserve all original uniform queries and the secant figure

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/interpolation_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/interpolation_search_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/binary_search.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto a = dm::algorithms::interpolation_example_values();
    std::print("| original key | found | index | interpolation probes | binary probes |\n| ---: | --- | " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int key : {13, 1, 19, 8, 20}) {
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::interpolation_search(a, key);
        const auto b = dm::algorithms::binary_search(a, key);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", key, r.found, r.index,
                   r.probes, b.probes);
    }
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::interpolation_search_trace(a, 13);
    auto p = dm::algorithms::interpolation_secant_plot(a, 13, t);
    p.title("Original uniform example: one actual probe").y_limits(0, 25).save_svg("build/interpolation_original.svg");
}
```
