> Preserve the skewed example and expose its intervals

For [1,2,…,9,1000] and key nine, the large final value repeatedly biases the
estimate toward lo. The actual search makes nine probes; the canonical binary
search makes three. The original uniform key-thirteen query instead takes four
binary probes. These are different measured queries, not a universal comparison.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/interpolation_skew.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/interpolation_search_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/binary_search.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto a = dm::algorithms::interpolation_example_values(true);
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::interpolation_search_trace(a, 9);
    std::print("| actual probe | lo | hi | candidate | value |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> step, width;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.probes.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &p = t.probes[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i + 1, p.lo, p.hi,
                   p.position, p.value);
        step.push_back(i + 1);
        width.push_back(p.hi - p.lo + 1);
    }
    std::print("Interpolation probes {}, binary probes {}.\n",
               t.run.result.probes, dm::algorithms::binary_search(a, 9).probes);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(step, width, "actual interval", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title(
        "Original skew: one-index progress").x_label("actual probe number").y_label("candidate interval length").y_limits(0, 13).save_svg("build/interpolation_skew.svg");
}
```
