> Worst-case work, distribution assumptions and boundary controls

A scalable missing-key control has n−1 zeros followed by n, and searches for
one. For any current lo before the final element, the offset is
floor((n−1−lo)/n)=0. The search visits each zero and then rejects the last
endpoint: exactly n−1 logical probes. Duplicated low values do not trigger the
flat shortcut because the interval's high endpoint differs. This is a
deterministic linear-work family.

An expected O(log log n) result instead needs assumptions about the key-value
distribution. Equal spacing is an exact special case, and neither that example
nor a selected skew establishes an empirical expected-time law. Interpolation
arithmetic and extra endpoint accesses also have costs beyond candidate counts;
no runtime crossover against binary search is claimed here.

Run/trace policies default to one million complete elements and actual retained
probe records each; zero disables either optional cap. The complete element cap
precedes reads. Trace admission precedes a candidate read/append, after that
iteration's endpoint guards have run. Untraced calls ignore the record cap;
legacy calls disable optional caps. Counters and index length are checked, and
errors return no partial trace. The borrowed array, scalar result/work, vector
capacity and byte/peak RAM are excluded from the record cap.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/interpolation_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/interpolation_search_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/binary_search.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::vector<double> sizes, linear, binary;
    std::print("| n | absent-key interpolation probes | binary probes |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512}) {
        const auto a = dm::algorithms::interpolation_adversary(n);
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::interpolation_search(a, 1);
        const auto b = dm::algorithms::binary_search(a, 1);
        if (r.found || r.probes != n - 1)
            throw std::runtime_error("linear family");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", n, r.probes, b.probes);
        sizes.push_back(n);
        linear.push_back(r.probes);
        binary.push_back(b.probes);
    }
    const std::vector<int> wide{std::numeric_limits<int>::min(), -1, 0, 1,
                                std::numeric_limits<int>::max()};
    for (int key : wide) {
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::interpolation_search(wide, key);
        if (!r.found || wide[r.index] != key)
            throw std::runtime_error("signed range");
    }
    std::print("All five full-signed-range targets verified.\n");
    const auto flat = dm::algorithms::interpolation_search_trace({2, 2, 2}, 2);
    std::print("Flat range: {} logical probe, {} candidate tests, first index {}.\n",
               flat.run.result.probes, flat.run.work.key_comparisons,
               flat.run.result.index);
    const auto skew = dm::algorithms::interpolation_example_values(true);
    (void) dm::algorithms::interpolation_search_trace(skew, 9, {10, 9});
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::interpolation_search_trace(skew, 9, {10, 8});
        throw std::runtime_error("cap accepted");
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        std::print("Exact nine-record cap admitted; cap eight rejected.\n");
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(sizes, linear, "interpolation", {220, 38, 38}, 2).line(sizes, binary,
                                                                  "binary",
                                                                  {37, 99, 235},
                                                                  2).title(
        "A measured linear worst-case family").x_label("input length n").y_label("logical candidate probes").y_limits(0, 650).save_svg("build/interpolation_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | absent-key interpolation probes | binary probes |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 4 | 3 | 3 |
| 8 | 7 | 4 |
| 16 | 15 | 5 |
| 32 | 31 | 6 |
| 64 | 63 | 7 |
| 128 | 127 | 8 |
| 256 | 255 | 9 |
| 512 | 511 | 10 |
All five full-signed-range targets verified.
Flat range: 1 logical probe, 0 candidate tests, first index 0.
Exact nine-record cap admitted; cap eight rejected.
\image(figures/interpolation_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Exact arithmetic protects the signed domain but does not remove distribution-sensitive worst-case work.)
\alttext(Absent-key controls produce n minus one interpolation probes while binary probe counts remain small; signed-range flat and budget checks execute separately.)

> Validation and reference

Native sanitizer checks compare sorted multisets and random arrays with
independent binary-search membership and exact widened candidate arithmetic,
inspecting every captured bound, value and selected counter. Full signed
extremes, flat/outside queries, original probes, ownership and
complete/one-short budgets are checked. Existing sorted-search and Eytzinger
consumers pass. Helpers validate complete fixture/display admission and bind
displayed candidates to their actual sources.

[NIST's interpolation-search entry|https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/interpolationSearch.html]
describes the uniform-distribution expectation and linear worst case, and cites
Yehoshua Perl, Alon Itai and Haim Avni, Interpolation search—a log logN search,
Communications of the ACM 21(7), 550–553, July 1978. This report's finite-int
implementation uses exact flooring, logical candidate counters and an unchecked
sorted-input contract. It does not claim that arbitrary almost-uniform arrays
satisfy a probabilistic theorem, or that arithmetic safety guarantees fewer
probes.

Independent Python Fraction checks verify 3498 cases and 3537 actual probe
records, including every selected counter and full signed extremes. Frozen safe
legacy cases preserve membership; 0 probe-count or duplicate-choice differences
occur among 3491 comparisons, consistent with the documented exact-floor
correction. Fresh coverage checks pass 37 definitions, 12 compiled public
examples and four clangd hovers.
